Create the Gateway and an initial (working) VirtualService so the application is reachable from outside the cluster:

cat <<EOF | oc apply -n bookinfo-mesh -f -
apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: Gateway
metadata:
  name: bookinfo-gateway
spec:
  selector:
    istio: ingressgateway
  servers:
  - port:
      number: 80
      name: http
      protocol: HTTP
    hosts:
    - "*"
---
apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: VirtualService
metadata:
  name: bookinfo
spec:
  hosts:
  - "*"
  gateways:
  - bookinfo-gateway
  http:
  - match:
    - uri:
        exact: /productpage
    - uri:
        prefix: /static
    - uri:
        exact: /login
    - uri:
        exact: /logout
    - uri:
        prefix: /api/v1/products
    route:
    - destination:
        host: productpage
        port:
          number: 9080
EOF
Verify the deployment and get the application URL:

# Check all pods are running (2/2 READY indicates the sidecar is injected)
oc get pods -n bookinfo-mesh

# Check services
oc get svc -n bookinfo-mesh

# Get the ingress gateway route and test the application
export INGRESS_HOST=$(oc get route istio-ingressgateway -n istio-system -o jsonpath='{.spec.host}')
export INGRESS_PORT=80
echo "Application URL: http://$INGRESS_HOST:$INGRESS_PORT/productpage"
curl -s "http://$INGRESS_HOST:$INGRESS_PORT/productpage" | grep -o "<title>.*</title>"
Deliverable 1.2: All pods in bookinfo-mesh show 2/2 READY (application container plus istio-proxy sidecar), and the curl command returns <title>Simple Bookstore App</title>. Save the output of oc get pods -n bookinfo-mesh as evidence.

Task 2: Implement Traffic Management
Subtask 2.1: Create Destination Rules and a Canary Deployment
Define destination rules for all four services, then configure a 90/10 weighted canary split between reviews v1 and v2.

cat <<EOF | oc apply -n bookinfo-mesh -f -
apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: DestinationRule
metadata:
  name: productpage
spec:
  host: productpage
  subsets:
  - name: v1
    labels:
      version: v1
---
apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: DestinationRule
metadata:
  name: reviews
spec:
  host: reviews
  trafficPolicy:
    loadBalancer:
      simple: LEAST_CONN
  subsets:
  - name: v1
    labels:
      version: v1
  - name: v2
    labels:
      version: v2
  - name: v3
    labels:
      version: v3
---
apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: DestinationRule
metadata:
  name: ratings
spec:
  host: ratings
  subsets:
  - name: v1
    labels:
      version: v1
---
apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: DestinationRule
metadata:
  name: details
spec:
  host: details
  subsets:
  - name: v1
    labels:
      version: v1
EOF

# Canary VirtualService: 90% of traffic to v1, 10% to v2
cat <<EOF | oc apply -n bookinfo-mesh -f -
apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: VirtualService
metadata:
  name: reviews
spec:
  hosts:
  - reviews
  http:
  - match:
    - headers:
        end-user:
          exact: jason
    route:
    - destination:
        host: reviews
        subset: v2
  - route:
    - destination:
        host: reviews
        subset: v1
      weight: 90
    - destination:
        host: reviews
        subset: v2
      weight: 10
EOF
Test the routing distribution:

# Expect roughly 9 of 10 requests to show no star rating (v1) and about 1 of 10 to show black stars (v2)
for i in {1..20}; do
  curl -s "http://$INGRESS_HOST:$INGRESS_PORT/productpage" | grep -o "glyphicon-star" | wc -l
  sleep 1
done

# Requests with the "jason" header should always be routed to v2 (black stars)
for i in {1..5}; do
  curl -s -H "end-user: jason" "http://$INGRESS_HOST:$INGRESS_PORT/productpage" | grep -o "glyphicon-star" | wc -l
  sleep 1
done
Deliverable 2.1: Record the count of star-rating occurrences across the 20 unweighted requests and the 5 jason-header requests. The unweighted run should show a roughly 90/10 split between "no rating" and "5 black stars," while every jason request should consistently return "5 black stars" (v2).

Subtask 2.2: Add Resilience Policies and Explain Circuit Breaker Behavior
Add a timeout/retry policy for reviews, and configure a circuit breaker (outlier detection) on the reviews DestinationRule.

# Add retries and timeout to the reviews route
cat <<EOF | oc apply -n bookinfo-mesh -f -
apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: VirtualService
metadata:
  name: reviews
spec:
  hosts:
  - reviews
  http:
  - match:
    - headers:
        end-user:
          exact: jason
    route:
    - destination:
        host: reviews
        subset: v2
  - route:
    - destination:
        host: reviews
        subset: v1
      weight: 90
    - destination:
        host: reviews
        subset: v2
      weight: 10
    timeout: 10s
    retries:
      attempts: 3
      perTryTimeout: 2s
EOF

# Add a circuit breaker (outlier detection) to the reviews DestinationRule
cat <<EOF | oc apply -n bookinfo-mesh -f -
apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: DestinationRule
metadata:
  name: reviews
spec:
  host: reviews
  trafficPolicy:
    loadBalancer:
      simple: LEAST_CONN
    connectionPool:
      tcp:
        maxConnections: 10
      http:
        http1MaxPendingRequests: 10
        maxRequestsPerConnection: 2
    outlierDetection:
      consecutiveErrors: 3
      interval: 30s
      baseEjectionTime: 30s
      maxEjectionPercent: 50
  subsets:
  - name: v1
    labels:
      version: v1
  - name: v2
    labels:
      version: v2
  - name: v3
    labels:
      version: v3
EOF
With this configuration, when a reviews pod returns 3 consecutive errors, Envoy ejects that pod instance from the load-balancing pool for baseEjectionTime (30 seconds). During the ejection window, all traffic is routed only to the remaining healthy reviews instances; the ejected pod is automatically returned to the pool once the ejection time expires, and if it fails again the ejection time doubles on subsequent trips. maxEjectionPercent: 50 guarantees that at least half of the instances always remain eligible to receive traffic, preventing a total outage of the service.

Confirm the policies were applied:

oc get destinationrule reviews -n bookinfo-mesh -o yaml | grep -A6 outlierDetection
oc get virtualservice reviews -n bookinfo-mesh -o yaml | grep -A3 timeout
Deliverable 2.2: A one-paragraph written note (in your lab journal or terminal history log) stating, in your own words, what happens to a reviews instance after 3 consecutive errors, how long it stays ejected, and why maxEjectionPercent matters. The oc get destinationrule output must show consecutiveErrors: 3 and baseEjectionTime: 30s.

Task 3: Security, Diagnosis, and Independent Troubleshooting
Subtask 3.1: Enable Mutual TLS and Authorization Policies
Configure strict mTLS for the namespace and lock down access to the ratings and details services so only their legitimate callers can reach them.

# Enforce strict mTLS across the namespace
cat <<EOF | oc apply -n bookinfo-mesh -f -
apiVersion: security.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: PeerAuthentication
metadata:
  name: default
spec:
  mtls:
    mode: STRICT
EOF

# Only allow the reviews service account to call ratings
cat <<EOF | oc apply -n bookinfo-mesh -f -
apiVersion: security.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: AuthorizationPolicy
metadata:
  name: ratings-policy
spec:
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: ratings
  rules:
  - from:
    - source:
        principals: ["cluster.local/ns/bookinfo-mesh/sa/bookinfo-reviews"]
    to:
    - operation:
        methods: ["GET"]
EOF

# Only allow the productpage service account to call details
cat <<EOF | oc apply -n bookinfo-mesh -f -
apiVersion: security.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: AuthorizationPolicy
metadata:
  name: details-policy
spec:
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: details
  rules:
  - from:
    - source:
        principals: ["cluster.local/ns/bookinfo-mesh/sa/bookinfo-productpage"]
    to:
    - operation:
        methods: ["GET"]
EOF
Verify the application still works end-to-end, and confirm that a service without an authorized principal is denied:

# Should still succeed (productpage -> details/reviews/ratings call chain is authorized)
curl -s "http://$INGRESS_HOST:$INGRESS_PORT/productpage" | grep -o "<title>.*</title>"

# Should be DENIED (productpage's own pod has no principal grant to call ratings directly)
oc exec -n bookinfo-mesh $(oc get pod -n bookinfo-mesh -l app=productpage -o jsonpath='{.items[0].metadata.name}') -c productpage -- curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\n" ratings:9080/ratings/1
Deliverable 3.1: The productpage curl returns the page title successfully, and the direct productpage to ratings call returns HTTP 403. Save both outputs.

Subtask 3.2: Diagnose and Independently Fix a Broken VirtualService
A teammate has just pushed a "hotfix" for the bookinfo VirtualService to add a new /api/v1/products header-based rule, but it broke external access to the application. Apply the broken configuration below exactly as written, then diagnose and repair it yourself.

cat <<EOF | oc apply -n bookinfo-mesh -f -
apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: VirtualService
metadata:
  name: bookinfo
spec:
  hosts:
  - "*"
  gateways:
  - bookinfo-gateway
  http:
  - match:
    - uri:
        prefix: /api/v1/products
      headers:
        x-debug:
          exact: "true"
    route:
    - destination:
        host: productpage
        port:
          number: 9080
EOF
Confirm the application is now broken:

curl -s -o /dev/null -w "HTTP status: %{http_code}\n" "http://$INGRESS_HOST:$INGRESS_PORT/productpage"
You should observe an HTTP status other than 200 (commonly 404). Do not simply reapply the original working YAML from Task 1 from memory â work through the diagnostic process below and produce your own fix.

Diagnostic steps to perform:

Inspect the currently applied VirtualService and compare its match block against the routes your Gateway needs to serve (/productpage, /static, /login, /logout, /api/v1/products):
oc get virtualservice bookinfo -n bookinfo-mesh -o yaml
Run Istio's built-in configuration analyzer to see if it flags the problem:
istioctl analyze -n bookinfo-mesh
Inspect the Envoy route configuration on the ingress gateway to confirm which paths are actually being routed:
istioctl proxy-config routes deploy/istio-ingressgateway -n istio-system --name http.80 -o json
Check the ingress gateway access logs for the rejected requests:
oc logs -n istio-system -l istio=ingressgateway --tail=50 | grep productpage
Your task: Based on what you find in steps 1-4, identify exactly which match conditions are missing or incorrectly scoped (consider: which URI paths no longer have a matching rule, and whether a header requirement is unintentionally restricting a route that should be open to all requests). Write and apply a corrected VirtualService for bookinfo that restores routing for /productpage, /static, /login, /logout, and unconditional /api/v1/products access, while still allowing the option of a separate debug-only rule if you choose to keep one. Do not copy a fix from elsewhere in this document verbatim; construct the http.match list yourself from the diagnostic evidence.

After applying your fix, verify the repair:

curl -s -o /dev/null -w "HTTP status: %{http_code}\n" "http://$INGRESS_HOST:$INGRESS_PORT/productpage"
curl -s "http://$INGRESS_HOST:$INGRESS_PORT/productpage" | grep -o "<title>.*</title>"
istioctl analyze -n bookinfo-mesh
Deliverable 3.2: A corrected bookinfo VirtualService YAML (written by you, not copy-pasted from Task 1) that restores /productpage, /static, /login, /logout, and /api/v1/products routing. The verification curl must return HTTP status: 200 and the page title, and istioctl analyze must report no errors for the bookinfo-mesh namespace. Include a short written note (2-3 sentences) describing which match condition was broken and why it caused the failure.

